      "writtenOn": "2025-01-29T01:00:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "oh I know why, because g_owner_data_var_name is char *, not char[], so sizeof() of it is sizeof(char *) \u003d 4 bytes (for a pointer). so actually shall not use sizeof with const char *.\n\nso need to do one of the three\n\n1) \n```\nconst char g_owner_data_var_name[] \u003d \"BPOD\";\n\n...\n\n// -1 is still needed then\nvar \u003d getvar(g_owner_data_var_name, sizeof(g_owner_data_var_name) - 1);\n```\n\n2)\n```\nconst char *g_owner_data_var_name \u003d \"BPOD\";\n\n...\n\nvar \u003d getvar(g_owner_data_var_name, strlen(g_owner_data_var_name));\n```\nnot sure if we will be calling strlen every single time.\n\n3)\n```\nconst char *g_owner_data_var_name \u003d \"BPOD\";\nconst size_t g_owner_data_var_name_len \u003d strlen(g_owner_data_var_name); // or just 4\n\n...\n\nvar \u003d getvar(g_owner_data_var_name, g_owner_data_var_name_len);\n```\n\nany approach is probably ok, even (2), even if we waste time on calling streln every time, it doesn\u0027t happen that often. codesize-wise, (1) or (3) might be slightly better if strlen on a const-string is not pre-calc\u0027ed at compile time.\n\nsame for g_auth_seed_var_name.",
      "parentUuid": "4f37eb55_a54f2d52",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 24,
        "startChar": 36,
